   "subject": "Re: Unidimensional vertical equating",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">The colnames(U) part is telling mirt which items to treat as anchors, and therefore set the item parameters to be equal across all groups. This is required for equating because this is essentially what is assumed during the traditional equating process: the item parameters are equal (no DIF), and only different in independent samples because the ability parameters differ. Is this how you set your simulation code up? <div><br><div><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Thu, Dec 27, 2018 at 5:15 PM Heliton Tavares &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">heliton...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">Hi <span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">Phil and members</span>,</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">I’m doing a\r\nsimulation study for unidimensional vertical equating, with:</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">1) 5 groups\r\nand 96 items (3PL). Each group (with 10000 individuals) answers to 24 items. Group means\r\n0,1,2,3,4 and sd 1, 0.8, 0.9, 1.1, 1.2</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">2) There are\r\n6 common items between adjacent groups (k and k+1).</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">3) Data U has\r\nNA to non answered items.</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">I’ve tried:</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">group= paste0(&quot;G&quot;,rep(1:5,\r\neach=10000))</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">multipleGroup(U,\r\n1, itemtype = &#39;3PL&#39;, group = group, <a name=\"m_1849549348250028972_m_-823164085765635362_OLE_LINK3\" rel=\"nofollow\"></a><a name=\"m_1849549348250028972_m_-823164085765635362_OLE_LINK2\" rel=\"nofollow\"></a><a name=\"m_1849549348250028972_m_-823164085765635362_OLE_LINK1\" rel=\"nofollow\">invariance=c(&#39;slopes&#39;, &#39;intercepts&#39;, &#39;free_var&#39;,&#39;free_means&#39;)</a>,\r\ntechnical = list(NCYCLES = 1000))</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">and with</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">invariance=c(<a name=\"m_1849549348250028972_m_-823164085765635362_OLE_LINK7\" rel=\"nofollow\">colnames(U),</a>&#39;slopes&#39;, <a name=\"m_1849549348250028972_m_-823164085765635362_OLE_LINK6\" rel=\"nofollow\"></a><a name=\"m_1849549348250028972_m_-823164085765635362_OLE_LINK5\" rel=\"nofollow\"></a><a name=\"m_1849549348250028972_m_-823164085765635362_OLE_LINK4\" rel=\"nofollow\">&#39;</a>intercepts&#39;,\r\n&#39;free_var&#39;,&#39;free_means&#39;)</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">The results\r\nare not fine for parameter recovery. Without <a name=\"m_1849549348250028972_m_-823164085765635362_OLE_LINK10\" rel=\"nofollow\"></a><a name=\"m_1849549348250028972_m_-823164085765635362_OLE_LINK9\" rel=\"nofollow\"></a><a name=\"m_1849549348250028972_m_-823164085765635362_OLE_LINK8\" rel=\"nofollow\">colnames(U) </a>the “c”\r\nparameters are different for each group, “a” and “b” are equals over the 5\r\ngroups, good estimates. Good abilities estimates too. But with colnames(U) all\r\nthe parameters estimates are the same <a name=\"m_1849549348250028972_m_-823164085765635362_OLE_LINK11\" rel=\"nofollow\">for the 5 groups</a>,\r\nbad estimates.</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">What I am\r\ndoing wrong? Do you have any good “suggestion”?</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><br></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Thanks in advance,</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Heliton Tavares</span></p></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div>"
